3. Kaggle "Gemma 4 Developer Agent" competition harness wheels:
   swegemma 0.2.11, adk-submission 0.2.13, adk-eval-core 0.1.0
   (Kaggle dataset metric/gemma-4-developer-agent-wheelhouse). This kit imports and wraps them
   (the grader calls swegemma's verify_task unmodified); it does not copy or modify their code.

4. Competition data: tasks.jsonl, snapshots/, wheels/, sandbox/setup.py, and
   docker/{Dockerfile.public, imp.py, telnetlib.py}, under the competition's rules.
   harness/sandbox/compose_dockerfile.py reads your downloaded Dockerfile.public at build time
   and only adds version pins; the file is not redistributed. The sandbox image you build
   contains the competition's two Python shims:
   - telnetlib.py is CPython's Lib/telnetlib.py (byte-identical to CPython 3.12's except that
     the module's deprecation-warning line is commented out). Copyright Python Software
     Foundation; licensed under the PSF License Agreement (Python-2.0),
     https://docs.python.org/3/license.html. CPython removed the module in 3.13 (PEP 594).
   - imp.py is a 26-line compatibility stub written for the competition (it provides
     `imp.find_module` via importlib for flasgger); it is not CPython's imp module.
   Neither shim is included in this distribution.

5. Model weights (Gemma 4, under the Gemma terms of use) and vLLM 0.19.1 (Apache-2.0) are
   downloaded separately and are not included, except that harness/repro/vllm_stub.py holds
   small stand-ins for a few vLLM 0.19.1 names (Apache-2.0).

6. Kaggle's official getting-started notebook: the driver constants and serving flags in
   harness/runner/core.py and harness/serve/serve.py follow it.
